 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 20,
   "y": 12,
   "text": "THE VILLAGE GREEN. A square of grass at the heart of Bushmills, kept in trim by a few hundred feet and the odd sheep who 'got in'. It has hosted every fair, concert and short argument for over two hundred years."
  },
  {
   "x": 28,
   "y": 28,
   "text": "THE OLD MILL. Bushmills is named for the mill on the river Bush: Muileann na Buaise, in Irish. A water mill has stood here for centuries, turning the river's weight into something useful and the walker's mood into 'oh, how quaint'."
  },
  {
   "x": 29,
   "y": 12,
   "text": "THE RIVER BUSH. A short, quick river full of salmon and trout, running down to the Causeway Coast. Anglers have cast here for centuries. The river has politely ignored them for just as long."
  },
  {
   "x": 3,
   "y": 15,
   "text": "BUSHMILLS. A small town with a river, a green, a steam train and a view. In 1883 the world's first hydro-electric tramway ran through here. The tram has gone. The feeling of being ahead of the times has not."
  },
  {
   "x": 8,
   "y": 15,
   "text": "BLUE PLAQUE: The Old Bushmills Distillery, along the road, has held a licence to distil since 1608.",
   "kind": "plaque",
   "dx": -0.475,
   "plaque": "bushmills_distillery",
   "title": "The Old Distillery"
  },
  {
   "x": 24,
   "y": 29,
   "plaque": "bushmills_uisce_beatha",
   "title": "Uisce Beatha",
   "text": "The word whiskey comes from the Irish uisce beatha, 'water of life'. English speakers kept the first half and lost the rest. Irish whiskey is usually spelt with an 'e', and Bushmills, like many Irish distilleries, distils its malt three times. The angels are said to take a share as it ages."
  },
  {
   "x": 35,
   "y": 16,
   "plaque": "bushmills_dunluce",
   "title": "Dunluce Castle",
   "text": "A few miles west, Dunluce Castle teeters on a basalt crag above the sea, joined to the land by a narrow bridge. It was a stronghold of the MacDonnells of Antrim in the 1500s and 1600s. Some say it helped inspire Cair Paravel in Narnia. It certainly has the sea views for it."
  },
  {
   "x": 22,
   "y": 29,
   "plaque": "bushmills_royal_portrush",
   "title": "Royal Portrush",
   "text": "Down the coast at Portrush is a links golf course among the sand dunes, where the wind does most of the scoring. In 1951 it became the first course outside England and Scotland to host the Open Championship, and the Open came back in 2019 and 2025."
  },
  {
   "x": 10,
   "y": 15,
   "plaque": "bushmills_ulster_scots",
   "title": "Ulster-Scots",
   "text": "North Antrim is a heartland of Ulster-Scots, the speech brought over by Scottish settlers in the 1600s. You'll hear 'wee' for small, 'thran' for stubborn and 'boke' for being sick. 'Aye' means yes, and 'och aye' means yes, with feeling."
  }
 ],
